.TH "COEPVIOLATIONREPORT" "3JS" "March 23, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
COEPViolationReport \- COEPViolationReport
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]COEPViolationReport\f[R] dictionary of the Reporting API
represents a report generated when a document violates its
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] (COEP).
.PP
Reports of this type can be observed from within a page using a
\f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R], or a serialized version can be sent to a
reporting server endpoint.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
.TP
\f[B]body\f[R]
The body of the report.
This is an object with the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
A string representing the cause of the violation that triggered the
report.
This can have one of the following values:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]\(dqcorp\(dq\f[R]
A document with \f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] set to
\f[CR]require\-corp\f[R] attempted to load a cross\-origin sub\-resource
that does not explicitly allow itself to be embedded (by setting an
appropriate \f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Resource\-Policy\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(dqnavigation\(dq\f[R]
An \f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] in a document that has either COEP
\f[CR]require\-corp\f[R] or \f[CR]credentialless\f[R] directives loads a
document that:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Has neither the COEP \f[CR]require\-corp\f[R] nor
\f[CR]credentialless\f[R] directive
.IP \(bu 2
Is cross\-origin with the embedding document, and does not have a CORP
header that allows embedding in the parent
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\(dqworker initialization\(dq\f[R]
A dedicated worker created by a document with either the COEP
\f[CR]require\-corp\f[R] or \f[CR]credentialless\f[R] directives tries
to load a worker script with neither of these set.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]blockedURL\f[R]
A string containing the URL of the resource that was blocked from
loading by an enforced COEP violation.
.TP
\f[B]destination\f[R] \f[I](non\-standard)\f[R]
A string indicating the \f[I]destination\f[R] of the blocked resource.
This can have one of the values of \f[CR]Request.destination\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]disposition\f[R]
A string indicating whether the violation was enforced or only reported.
This can have one of the following values:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]\(dqenforce\(dq\f[R]
The violation caused loading of the embedded resource to be blocked.
This is set for violations of policies set with
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\(dqreporting\(dq\f[R]
The violation was reported without blocking the resource from loading.
This is set for violations of policies set with
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\-Report\-Only\f[R].
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
The string \f[CR]\(dqcoep\(dq\f[R], indicating that this is a COEP
violation report.
.TP
\f[B]url\f[R]
A string representing the URL of the document that generated the report.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A document\(cqs policies for loading and embedding cross\-origin
resources that are requested in \f[CR]no\-cors\f[R] mode are configured
and enforced using the \f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] HTTP
header, and may also be reported but not enforced using the
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\-Report\-Only\f[R] header.
.PP
COEP policy violations may be reported whenever a policy set by those
headers blocks (or would block) the loading of a resource.
.PP
You can monitor for COEP violation reports within the page that sets the
policy using the Reporting API.
To do this you create a \f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R] object to listen
for reports, passing a callback method and an (optional)
\f[CR]options\f[R] property specifying the types of reports that you
want to report on.
The callback method is then called with reports of the requested types,
passing a report object.
For COEP violations, the object will be a \f[CR]COEPViolationReport\f[R]
(which has the \f[CR]type\f[R] property set to \f[CR]\(dqcoep\(dq\f[R]).
.PP
The structure of a typical report is shown below.
Note that we can see the URL of both the page that had its policy
violated (\f[CR]url\f[R]) and the resource that was blocked from loading
(\f[CR]body.blockedURL\f[R]).
We can also see that the report was triggered by a \f[CR]corp\f[R]
violation, and from the \f[CR]body.disposition\f[R] that it was enforced
(and not just reported).
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcoep\(dq,
  \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://url\-of\-page\-attempting\-to\-load\-resource\-in\-violation\(dq,
  \(dqbody\(dq: {
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcorp\(dq,
    \(dqblockedURL\(dq: \(dqhttps://url\-of\-blocked\-resource\(dq,
    \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqimage\(dq,
    \(dqdisposition\(dq: \(dqenforce\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.PP
Violation reports may also be sent as a JSON object in a \f[CR]POST\f[R]
to a configured reporting server endpoint.
The reporting server endpoint name is specified in the
\f[CR]report\-to\f[R] policy directive of the
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] or
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\-Report\-Only\f[R] header.
Valid endpoint names and their mapping to a particular URL are defined
using the \f[CR]Reporting\-Endpoints\f[R] header.
.PP
The structure of the server report is almost exactly the same as
\f[CR]COEPViolationReport\f[R], except that it additionally includes
\f[CR]age\f[R] and \f[CR]user_agent\f[R] fields.
.IP
.EX
[
  {
    \(dqage\(dq: 967132,
    \(dqbody\(dq: {
      \(dqblockedURL\(dq: \(dqhttps://url\-of\-resource\-that\-was\-blocked\(dq,
      \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqimage\(dq,
      \(dqdisposition\(dq: \(dqenforce\(dq,
      \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcorp\(dq
    },
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcoep\(dq,
    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://url\-of\-document\-that\-generated\-report\(dq,
    \(dquser_agent\(dq: \(dqMozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/143.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\(dq
  }
]
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using the \f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R] interface
This example shows how you can obtain COEP violation reports using a
\f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R].
.PP
First consider the case where we have an HTML file hosted on the origin
\f[CR]https://example.com\f[R], which includes an \f[CR]<img>\f[R]
element that sets as its source the (cross\-origin) resource
\f[CR]some\-image.png\f[R].
Since the element does not set the \f[CR]crossorigin\f[R] attribute, it
will be requested in \f[CR]no\-cors\f[R] mode.
By default, if \f[CR]some\-image.png\f[R] is not served with the
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] header, this request will
succeed.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]img\f[R] src=\(dqhttps://another\-example.com/some\-image.png\(dq />
.EE
.PP
In order to ensure that the document only loads cross\-origin resources
that indicate that they are safe to load in our document origin, we can
set the \f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\f[R] header with the
\f[CR]require\-corp\f[R] directive as shown:
.IP
.EX
Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy: require\-corp
.EE
.PP
This header enforces that all resources must be served with the
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Resource\-Policy\f[R] header and a value of
\f[CR]cross\-origin\f[R] in order to be loaded into the document\(cqs
origin (\f[CR]https://example.com\f[R]).
Provided the server hosting \f[CR]some\-image.png\f[R] doesn\(cqt set
the header, we don\(cqt need to do anything else to trigger a COEP
violation.
.PP
To observe violations within the page, we construct a new
\f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R] object to listen for reports with the type
\f[CR]\(dqcoep\(dq\f[R], passing a callback that will receive and log
the reports.
This code needs to be loaded before the script that causes the
violation:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] options = {
  types: [\(dqcoep\(dq],
  buffered: \f[B]true\f[R],
};

\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] ReportingObserver((reports, observer) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  reports.forEach((violation) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    console.log(violation);
    console.log(JSON.stringify(violation));
  });
}, options);

observer.observe();
.EE
.PP
Above, we log each violation report object and a JSON\-string version of
the object, which might look similar to the object below.
Note that the \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqcoep\(dq\f[R].
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcoep\(dq,
  \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://example.com\(dq,
  \(dqbody\(dq: {
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcorp\(dq,
    \(dqblockedURL\(dq: \(dqhttps://another\-example.com/some\-image.png\(dq,
    \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqimage\(dq,
    \(dqdisposition\(dq: \(dqenforce\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.PP
The same report could be generated using
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\-Report\-Only\f[R], except that
the disposition would be reported as \f[CR]\(dqreporting\(dq\f[R].
.SS Sending a report to a reporting endpoint
Configuring a web page to send a COEP report to a reporting server
endpoint is almost the same as the previous example.
The only difference is that we need to specify a reporting endpoint
where we want the reports to be sent, using the
\f[CR]Reporting\-Endpoints\f[R] response header, and then reference
these in the \f[CR]report\-to\f[R] parameter when setting the policy.
.PP
You can see this below, where we define the endpoint named
\f[CR]coep\-endpoint\f[R] and then reference it in our policy:
.IP
.EX
Reporting\-Endpoints: coep\-endpoint=\(dqhttps://some\-example.com/coep\(dq
Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy: require\-corp; report\-to=\(dqcoep\-endpoint\(dq
.EE
.PP
The violation report will then be sent as a JSON object in a
\f[CR]POST\f[R] to the endpoint referenced by \f[CR]coep\-endpoint\f[R].
.PP
The report object has the same structure as returned from the
\f[CR]ReportingObserver\f[R] callback except for the addition of
\f[CR]age\f[R] and \f[CR]user_agent\f[R] properties.
.IP
.EX
[
  {
    \(dqage\(dq: 717139,
    \(dqbody\(dq: {
      \(dqblockedURL\(dq: \(dqhttps://another\-example.com/some\-image.png\(dq,
      \(dqdestination\(dq: \(dqimage\(dq,
      \(dqdisposition\(dq: \(dqenforce\(dq,
      \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcorp\(dq
    },
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqcoep\(dq,
    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://example.com\(dq,
    \(dquser_agent\(dq: \(dqMozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/143.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\(dq
  }
]
.EE
.PP
The same report would be generated if we set
\f[CR]Cross\-Origin\-Embedder\-Policy\-Report\-Only\f[R] in the same
way, except that the disposition would be set to
\f[CR]\(dqreporting\(dq\f[R].
